[38;5;231m⏺[39m Before I compare the two truncation strategies, one thing changes what both of them compute.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153m'...'[39m fits [3minside[0m [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m or is appended [3mon top of[0m it. With [38;5;153mformat("hello world", { truncate: 8 [39m
  [38;5;153m})[39m:

  - [1mEllipsis inside the budget[0m → [38;5;153m"hello..."[39m (8 chars). The result is never longer than [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, so the option is a
    hard guarantee you can rely on for column widths, table cells, or DB fields. Costs you 3 characters of real content,
    and it needs an edge-case rule for [38;5;153mmaxLength < 3[39m.
  - [1mEllipsis on top[0m → [38;5;153m"hello wo..."[39m (11 chars). You keep the full [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m of content, but the output can exceed
    [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m by 3, so it can't be used as a width guarantee.

  This one is expensive to change later — it's the observable contract of the option, and anything that depends on the
  width would break if it flips. The word-boundary choice you raised is cheap to change by comparison; it only shifts
  where the cut lands.

  I recommend [1mellipsis inside the budget[0m: "max length" reading as an actual maximum is what callers assume, and it's the
  only version that's safe to point at a fixed-width target.
